
                                                    CHECKSUM COMMAND


Performs CHECKSUM operations

Below is the syntax :
          CHECKSUM_ALGORITHM_NAME -startAddress [start address] -endAddress [end address] 
                -startValue [start value] -endValue [end value]  
                -alignment [alignment flag] -inverse [inverse flag]
                -outputAddress [output address] -outputtxt [output text filane name with path]

-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SWITCHES               |    DESCRIPTION
-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   -startAddress       |    Start address of data range. Mandatory switch. 
   -endAddress         |    End address of data range. Mandatory Switch.  
   -startValue         |    Start Value for calculating checksum. Optional switch.
   -endValue           |    End Value for calculating checksum. Optional switch.
   -inverse            |    Inverse flag which is a boolean attribute. Optional switch.
   -alignement         |    Alignement flag which is a boolean attribute. Optional switch.
   -outputaddress      |    Output address where the calculated checksum is patched. Optional switch.
   -outputtxt          |    Text file to be generated which contains the calculated checksum for given range. 
                       |    Optional switch.
-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Example:
    1) read -in Core.hex -igCks true 
             CRC32  -inverse  true –alignment true -startAddress 0x80820000 -endAddress 0x8082001F 
             -startValue 0xFADACAFE  -endValue 0xFADACAFE 
             -outputtxt chksum_hexmodX.txt   -outputaddress  0x80820040
             
    2) read -in Core.hex -igCks true 
             CRC64  -inverse  true –alignment true -startAddress 0x80820000 -endAddress 0x8082001F 
             -startValue 0xFADACAFE  -endValue 0xFADACAFE 
             -outputtxt chksum_hexmodX.txt   -outputaddress  0x80820040
             
Checksum value can also be calculated for multiple ranges.

Example:    
     
    1)  read -in Core.hex -igCks true 
            ADD8 -startAddress 0x80820000 -endAddress 0x8082001F 
           -startAddress 0x80820020 -endAddress 0x8082003F  
           -outputtxt chksum_hexmodX.txt   -outputaddress  0x80820040
        
Note that checksum value of first data range is considered as start value of second data range. Similarly for successive data ranges. 
